# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two FILES, never a
# shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
# Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_cochange_min_support.py" "COCHANGE-MIN-SUPPORT GATE: PASS" "co-change min_support raised 3->5 (annotation-sweep FP elimination)" "co-change MIN-SUPPORT=5: raises the support floor from 3 to 5, eliminating the annotation/format-sweep false-positive class identified in a Flask dogfood precision audit (support=3: 399 pairs / 220 low-support FPs on Flask window=800; support=5: 179 pairs / 0 low-support FPs; recall cost = only pairs co-changed 3-4 times in 800 commits, a weak signal by definition). The FP mechanism: a tooling-sweep commit touching <=40 files (so NOT skipped by the giant-commit guard) inflates lift for rarely-changed file pairs -- both files have n_a=n_b=co=3 so strength=1.0 (passes the render-layer min_prob=0.4 filter) AND lift=co*N/(n_a*n_b)>>1 (both files are rare movers so chance baseline is tiny -- looks like a real coupling). The ONLY layer that can gate these out is the support floor. Proven on synthetic commit fixtures: a sweep FP with co=4 is DROPPED at default support=5 but survives at explicit support=3 (backwards-compat confirmed); a genuine coupling with co=5 is KEPT; callers that pin support=3 explicitly see no change (all existing co-change gates pass unchanged). Content-free, bounded, never-crash -- none of those properties affected" "co-change min_support raised 3->5: eliminates annotation-sweep FP class (str=1.0 pairs that bypass the render-layer min_prob filter) while keeping genuine couplings with co>=5; callers pinning support=3 explicitly are unaffected" 10
